JavaScript's built-in Array.prototype.shuffle() method does not exist. However, you can use the Fisher-Yates shuffle algorithm to shuffle an array in place.

Here's an example of how you can implement the Fisher-Yates shuffle algorithm:

```javascript
function shuffleArray(arr) {
  for (let i = arr.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
    const j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));
    [arr[i], arr[j]] = [arr[j], arr[i]];
  }
  return arr;
}
```

This function shuffles the array in place, meaning it modifies the original array without returning a new one.

If you want to return a new shuffled array, you can modify the function like this:

```javascript
function shuffleArray(arr) {
  const shuffled = arr.slice();
  for (let i = shuffled.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
    const j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));
    [shuffled[i], shuffled[j]] = [shuffled[j], shuffled[i]];
  }
  return shuffled;
}
```

In this version, we first create a copy of the original array using the Array.prototype.slice() method. We then shuffle the copy and return it.
